[
  {"id": "question 8798", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 5, "evidence": "TS 38.401 §8.2.2.3 defines the Inter-gNB-DU Conditional PSCell Change using MCG SRB without MN negotiation procedure. Step 5 states: 'The gNB-CU sends an UE CONTEXT SETUP REQUEST message to the candidate gNB-DU to create an UE context and setup one or more data bearers.' Option 5 'establish a new RRC connection for the UE in the target gNB-DU' is the closest match to setting up the UE context/bearers in the target gNB-DU. Although technically RRC resides at the MCG/MN level, the procedure does establish the UE's connection (UE context) at the target gNB-DU.", "source": "TS 38.401 v17.10.0 §8.2.2.3"},
  {"id": "question 8808", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 5, "evidence": "TS 23.002 §7.6 explicitly states: '7.6 Reference point PDG – packet data networks (Wi reference point) This is the reference point between the PDG and a packet data network.' Option 5 'Wi reference point' matches exactly.", "source": "TS 23.002 §7.6"},
  {"id": "question 8833", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 3, "evidence": "TS 38.321 §5.7 states: 'When RRC does not configure a secondary DRX group, there is only one DRX group and all Serving Cells belong to that one DRX group.' This is the default configuration. Options 1 and 5 (cell belongs to multiple groups) are false — each Serving Cell is uniquely assigned. Option 2 (each cell has own group) is false — cells can share a group.", "source": "TS 38.321 §5.7"},
  {"id": "question 9013", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 5, "evidence": "TS 22.011 §4.6 is titled exactly 'Prevention of mobile-originating signalling and/or data traffic' and states: 'The network shall be able to control the behavior of UEs in E-UTRAN in connected mode to prevent mobile originating signalling and/or data traffic, while the access barring mechanisms specified under Clause 4.3 are being applied to UEs in idle mode.' This matches option 5 verbatim. SSAC (§4.3.2) applies in both idle and connected mode but §4.6 is the specific mechanism described for connected-mode UE control.", "source": "TS 22.011 v18 §4.6"},
  {"id": "question 9014", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 4, "evidence": "TS 29.520 §4.3.1.3.2 (NF Service Consumers — PCF) explicitly states: 'The Policy Control Function (PCF): ... supports taking analytics information for network performance from the NWDAF'. PCF is listed as a known consumer of Nnwdaf_AnalyticsInfo for the Network Performance analytics ID.", "source": "TS 29.520 §4.3.1.3.2"},
  {"id": "question 9050", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 1, "evidence": "TS 23.288 §4.2.0 explicitly defines: 'The Nnf interface is defined for the NWDAF to request subscription to data delivery for a particular context, to cancel subscription to data delivery and to request a specific report of data for a particular context.' This is exactly option 1 'Nnf interface'.", "source": "TS 23.288 §4.2.0"},
  {"id": "question 9183", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 4, "evidence": "BSS (Base Station Subsystem) consists of BSC + BTS in GSM/GPRS. Per TS 43.050 and 3GPP BSS architecture description, BSS is responsible for the radio access network connecting MS to GERAN — i.e., 'establishing means of connection between an MS and the GERAN' (option 4). The other options describe sub-functions: option 1 (BSC radio resource integrity), option 2 (BSC radio resource allocation), option 3 (BSC terminates Abis toward BTS), option 5 (BTS transmission/reception control).", "source": "TS 43.050; 3GPP BSS architecture"},
  {"id": "question 9237", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 2, "evidence": "TS 32.296 §6.2.2.2.2 ('TariffRequest scenario with service denial or unsuccessful delivery') describes the reservation failure flow: 'Step 12: The SBCF performs final account control (i.e. releases account balance reservations)'. Step 9: SBCF sends TariffRequest with release instruction. The reserved (unused) units are returned back to the subscriber account, matching option 2 'The OCS returns the reserved amount back to the subscriber account'.", "source": "TS 32.296 §6.2.2.2.2"},
  {"id": "question 9277", "verdict": "unverifiable", "correct_option": null, "evidence": "I could not locate a definitive 3GPP standard text that uniquely matches one of the options. Behavior when the network cannot provide required QoS for a data service depends on the service type (bearer-level PDP/PDU session activation typically fails per TS 24.008/24.501; IMS sessions can be informed per TS 23.228). Option 4 ('service will not be provided') fits the bearer case; option 2 ('both applications informed') fits IMS. Without a single definitive source for the generic 'data service' phrasing, no verdict can be given confidently.", "source": "own knowledge (TS 23.107, TS 23.228, TS 24.008, TS 24.501 reviewed)"},
  {"id": "question 9359", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 2, "evidence": "TS 23.288 §4.1 states: 'An Analytics ID information element is used to identify the type of supported analytics that NWDAF can generate.' And §5.1: 'Different NWDAF instances may be present in the 5GC, with possible specializations per type of analytics.' NWDAF generates only the specialized/3GPP-defined analytics types, not arbitrary ones. Option 2 ('Only specialized types of analytics') is correct.", "source": "TS 23.288 §4.1, §5.1"},
  {"id": "question 9516", "verdict": "wrong", "correct_option": 1, "evidence": "Per TS 23.122 §3.1 and §4.4.3.1, the purpose of PLMN selection is to enable the UE to select a PLMN to register on. Option 1 'To identify the selected PLMN' correctly describes the selection purpose. Option 4 'To report available PLMNs to NAS on request' describes reporting (a NAS procedure) — not PLMN selection itself. Option 2 is cell selection; option 5 is cell selection for camping.", "source": "TS 23.122 §3.1, §4.4.3.1"},
  {"id": "question 9664", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 1, "evidence": "IEEE 802.11-2016 §17.3.10.5 (CCA for 802.11a, OFDM PHY): 'The start of a valid OFDM transmission at a receive level equal to or greater than the minimum 6-Mb/s sensitivity (-82 dBm) shall cause CCA to indicate busy with a probability > 90% within 4 µs. If the preamble portion was missed, the receiver shall hold the carrier sense (CS) signal busy for any signal 20 dB above the minimum 6-Mb/s sensitivity.' CCA is determined by RSS thresholds — option 1 is correct.", "source": "IEEE 802.11-2016 §17.3.10.5"},
  {"id": "question 9728", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 4, "evidence": "TS 23.012 §2.1: 'Location management means that the PLMNs keep track of where the MSs are located in the system area.' The purpose is to track UE location across the system area so that service delivery can be maintained regardless of UE location — i.e., 'To provide continuity of service to UEs over the whole system area' (option 4).", "source": "TS 23.012 §2.1"},
  {"id": "question 9780", "verdict": "wrong", "correct_option": null, "evidence": "Per TS 24.008 §4.7.5.1, in state GMM-REGISTERED.NORMAL-SERVICE the MS performs normal operations including cell reselection, sending user data, responding to paging, transmitting signalling, AND initiating normal RAU when triggered (e.g., 'when the MS enters GMM-REGISTERED.NORMAL-SERVICE and the TIN indicates GUTI'). All five 'Not…' options describe behaviors the MS does exhibit in NORMAL-SERVICE — therefore none are correct. The question is malformed. The marked answer (option 3 'Not initiate normal routing area updating') is wrong because the MS does initiate RAU in NORMAL-SERVICE.", "source": "TS 24.008 §4.7.5.1"},
  {"id": "question 9831", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 2, "evidence": "Per IEEE 802.11 architecture: the Distribution System (DS) is the architectural component that interconnects BSSs (Basic Service Sets) to form an Extended Service Set (ESS). Its purpose is to enable station mobility between APs/BSSs. Option 2 'Providing mobility by connecting access points' is the standard description of DS function. Option 3 (delivering frames to right AP) is a sub-function; options 1 and 4 are not DS functions.", "source": "IEEE 802.11-2016 §4 (Architecture)"},
  {"id": "question 9834", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 4, "evidence": "TS 38.321 §5.1.5 (Contention Resolution) for initial access: 'if the MAC PDU contains a UE Contention Resolution Identity MAC CE; and if the UE Contention Resolution Identity in the MAC CE matches the CCCH SDU transmitted in Msg3'. The UE Contention Resolution Identity is a 48-bit random value selected by the UE in Msg3 and echoed by the gNB in Msg4. Option 4 'Random value for contention resolution' matches. Temporary C-RNTI (option 3) is used for addressing but not the identity used in contention resolution matching.", "source": "TS 38.321 §5.1.5"},
  {"id": "question 9953", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 4, "evidence": "Per IEEE 802.1D-2004 (and IEEE 802.1Q), spanning-tree port attributes are categorized into Roles (Root, Designated, Alternate, Backup, Master) and States (Disabled, Blocking, Listening, Learning, Forwarding in STP; Discarding, Learning, Forwarding in RSTP). If 'status' is interpreted as role/status (the typical STP terminology), then ROOT, DESIGNATED, ALTERNATE are all valid roles, while FORWARDING is a state — not a role/status. Thus FORWARDING is NOT a 'status' (in the sense of a port role). Option 4 is correct.", "source": "IEEE 802.1D-2004 §17"}
]